  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nMain focus of forecast remains on transition from muggy and at times \nwet conditions to a much more pleasant period later Thursday through \nthe weekend and maybe beyond. \n\nSort of a disjointed setup across the region with models continuing \nto struggle on handling of current precip and evolution into the \nremainder of the afternoon and evening hours. Small lines of showers \nhave attempted to develop ahead of the band of rain from portions \nof Lower MI to near Lk MI and points SW where pockets of clearing \nand weak convergence gives just enough to pop the showers. By in \nlarge these have weakened with the greatest potential area most \nlikely bisecting the CWA from SW to NE. Ahead of the, SPC Meso shows \nlimited instability (500-1500 J/KG MUCAPE) but best shear displaced \nbehind the instability. While models disagree on current handling of \nkey features, they all seem to have some semblance of agreement on \nbetter potential for strong to severe storms just outside our area \nfrom portions of NY and OH to the SW. Newest SPC DY1 outlook reflects \nthis with a further trimming on the NW fringe of the Marginal Risk \narea. Some sort of pops will be needed until the trough settles just \noutside the area and becomes parallel to the flow and likely washes \nout. The main cold front isn't too far away (central U.P of MI to \neastern IA) but is going to move little until getting a better push \nfrom the upper level trough moving in later tonight into Thursday. \nThis trough may generate some additional storms across portions of \nMO and IL and could track along the boundary with a small chance of \nsurviving into our far southern counties. Have left pops very \nsuppressed for now. \n\nBeyond Thursday, high pressure takes over to bring much more \npleasant (and dry) conditions to the area through most, if not all \nof the weekend. The upper level flow begins to shift once again next \nweek with heights increasing aloft in response to a wave pushing \nacross the Dakotas early next week. Models seem to agree on the bulk \nof the energy from this being kept away from the area, but model \nblend have thrown in periods of slgt chc to chc pops with the \ngreatest focus towards mid week.",
